train.user:
How does the MATLAB code below simulate the trajectory of a Tesla Model 3 using advanced system dynamics and control techniques?
% Define the Tesla Model 3 vehicle parameters
m = 1610; % mass of the car (kg)
A = 2.3; % frontal area of the car (m^2)
Cd = 0.23; % drag coefficient of the car
Cr = 0.005; % rolling resistance coefficient of the car
Fmax = 2200; % maximum force generated by the electric motor (N)
% Define the longitudinal dynamics of the car
f = @(t,x,u) [x(2); (Fmax*u-Cd*A*x(2)^2/2-(m*9.81*Cr))/m];
% Define the initial conditions and time span
x0 = [0; 0];
tspan = [0 100];
% Simulate the trajectory of the car with various inputs
u1 = @(t) 0.3*Fmax; % constant throttle input
[t1,x1] = ode45(@(t,x) f(t,x,u1(t)), tspan, x0);
u2 = @(t) Fmax*(1-exp(-t/10)); % ramp throttle input
[t2,x2] = ode45(@(t,x) f(t,x,u2(t)), tspan, x0);
u3 = @(t) Fmax*(1-exp(-t/10)).*(t<50)+Fmax*(1-exp(-50/10)).*(t>=50); % step throttle input
[t3,x3] = ode45(@(t,x) f(t,x,u3(t)), tspan, x0);
% Plot the results
figure;
subplot(2,1,1); plot(t1,x1(:,1),t2,x2(:,1),t3,x3(:,1)); title('Position vs. Time'); xlabel('Time (s)'); ylabel('Position (m)'); legend('Constant Throttle','Ramp Throttle','Step Throttle');
subplot(2,1,2); plot(t1,x1(:,2),t2,x2(:,2),t3,x3(:,2)); title('Velocity vs. Time'); xlabel('Time (s)'); ylabel('Velocity (m/s)'); legend('Constant Throttle','Ramp Throttle','Step Throttle'); 
Note: The code simulates the trajectory of a Tesla Model 3 using advanced system dynamics and control techniques.

